 now mounts his offensive against  his rival kenny has also been gathering  his team together his chief of staff  recently took a leave of absence to  start campaigning i'm not confident i'm  hopeful people  are angry and when people are angry it  motivates them  i'm hoping cooler heads prevail this  political analyst says there's no doubt  albertus premier is in a fight for his  job when the leadership review was first  announced i was convinced that somehow  by hook or by crook jason kelly would  pull this out but there's more and more  evidence coming out that he's in real  danger that fate now rests in the hands  of party members who will decide if they  want to keep their leader the vote is  set for april 9th in red deer jamie dahl  global news calgary  just ahead the federal conservative  candidate who could be a king maker  the race to become the next leader of  canada's conservative party is growing  ontario mp leslie lewis has thrown her  hat into the ring again she placed third  in the party's last leadership race  buoyed by social conservatives but as  our chief political correspondent david  aiken reports lewis's goal now is to  broaden that support in a race that's  very different from the last one  she seemed to come out of nowhere in  2020 finishing a strong third behind  peter mckay and winner aaron o'toole and  since then leslie lewis won a seat in  the 2021 general election and now she  wants another crack the top job i'm  concerned about our 1.3 trillion dollar  debt i'm concerned about the divisions  and the demonization  and just the  the polarization that i see taking place  amongst politicians in 2020 lewis's  support was heavily concentrated in  western canada to win this time she'll  have to expand that base to vote rich  ontario and quebec her chief challenge  in quebec will be just as it was in 2020  an inability to speak french and to  party activists like yon plant who is a  senior aide to a harper-era quebec  cabinet minister speaking both official  languages is a non-negotiable  requirement for any national leader how  can you win an election i mean it's  impossible  right it's just mathematics it does  it doesn't add up  so so there's there's two at least two  angles to it there's one of respect and  there's one that strategically just  doesn't make sense another challenge  perceived front-runner pierre pogliev is  also likely to be attractive to many of  the western-based and social  conservatives who backed lewis in 2020.  this polyeff supporter who also served  as a top aide to harper era ministers  believes a lewis candidacy is very  different this time around  you know now that she's a newly elected  mp uh with a public record both in her  writing and her speeches in the house of  commons and her votes her campaign is  going to need to find a way to create  that same buzz or enthusiasm  now notably while pauliev patrick brown  and jean-charade have already taken  turns attacking each other  no one has taken aim at lewis and that's  perhaps a quiet recognition that just  like in 2020 one of them may need to win  over lewis's followers to put them over  the top when the race concludes in  september david aiken global news ottawa  tragic parallels up next how history is  repeating for ukrainian who remembers  have a look at this brave little guy  this jack russell terrier is reportedly  helping anti-bomb crews sniff out and  clear explosives in ukraine according to  social media posts by the state  emergency service of ukraine the doc has  helped deactivate almost 90 devices  since the invasion began  for most ukrainians this war began eight  years ago with russia's annexation of  crimea how it has unfolded is  unimaginable for most but not all as  mike armstrong reports for some this is  the second time they've lived through  the horrors of heartless aggression  there is a disbelief in this voice  margarita morozova is living through  horrors that shouldn't be but are  familiar  in my worst nightmare i couldn't even  imagine that such a massacre would be  repeated she says it's horrible  the 87 year old morozova lives in the  city of harkief it's just 25 kilometers  from the russian border for weeks it's  faced an almost constant assault from  air and rocket strikes  morzova's family has put plastic up over  the windows in case they shatter  and they have a plan when the bombings  get too close they all huddle together  in the hallway although she admits she's  not sure it's actually any safer  she says it's what she did as a child  the first time she lived in a city under  siege  morizova was just seven years old when  german soldiers attacked leningrad  russia the city now called saint  petersburg she tried to escape before  the city was encircled but she and her  mother missed a ferry a ferry that was  hit and sunk as it left  leningrad was cut off for  872 days  between the shelling and the starvation  1.5 million people were killed  at the time rationing meant morozova  survived on just a few slices of bread  each day a bread made of flour and  sawdust she remembers the famine she  says  and her mother telling her the scariest  thing was looking into your child's  hungry eyes and not being able to feed  them  morizova has lived in ukraine for more  than 60 years she has a daughter in her  kiev and a son in russia  that's where that disbelief comes back  the two countries are too close for this  she says their cultures and languages  are similar and she doesn't understand  how it's possible  morozova hopes for a quick end to this  war buildings can be rebuilt she says  but people who die can't be brought back  and then there's what the war is  creating something she calls a disaster  a hatred  between neighbors  mike armstrong global news jeshof poland  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm colleen christie it's  been a long winter across canada but  today officially marks the first day of  spring so we leave you tonight with  these beautiful cherry blossoms that  have just bloomed in bc's greater  vancouver area thanks for watching  jackson proska will be at the anchor  desk tomorrow have a good night
 

